WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:09.295
Eight ledger lookups go through JDBC, and each one blocks its thread for 100 milliseconds.

2
00:00:09.295 --> 00:00:18.747
You add subscribeOn(boundedElastic) to get them off the caller's thread, and they still take 800 milliseconds, one after another.

3
00:00:18.747 --> 00:00:19.040
Why?

4
00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:22.886
Without a scheduler, Reactor doesn't switch threads.

5
00:00:22.886 --> 00:00:28.113
You call subscribe from the main thread, and that call travels up the chain to the source.

6
00:00:28.113 --> 00:00:33.049
The source emits, and each item flows down on the thread that emitted it: here, main.

7
00:00:33.267 --> 00:00:36.076
Now put publishOn(single) in the middle.

8
00:00:36.076 --> 00:00:37.269
Which steps move?

9
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:42.027
publishOn is a hand-off point.

10
00:00:42.027 --> 00:00:46.209
Items wait in its queue, and a worker of single carries them on.

11
00:00:46.209 --> 00:00:47.908
Everything after it moves.

12
00:00:47.908 --> 00:00:49.672
Everything before it stays.

13
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.029
Now add subscribeOn(boundedElastic), written near the end.

14
00:00:54.029 --> 00:00:55.524
What moves this time?

15
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.355
subscribeOn works on the way up.

16
00:01:00.355 --> 00:01:05.556
The subscribe call starts on main, and switches to boundedElastic at subscribeOn.

17
00:01:05.556 --> 00:01:09.601
So the source runs there, although subscribeOn is written last.

18
00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:12.812
After publishOn, the items still change to single.

19
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:16.914
Now swap publishOn for a second subscribeOn, this one on single.

20
00:01:16.914 --> 00:01:21.644
The call switches twice on the way up, and the switch nearest the source wins.

21
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:24.847
Back to the lookups.

22
00:01:24.847 --> 00:01:29.087
fromCallable runs its JDBC call the moment it is subscribed.

23
00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:38.557
With subscribeOn on the outer Flux, flatMap subscribes to every call on that one boundedElastic thread, so the calls block it in turn.

24
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.077
Move subscribeOn onto each inner Mono.

25
00:01:44.077 --> 00:01:51.065
Each subscription now hops to its own boundedElastic thread: about 100 milliseconds for all eight.

26
00:01:51.867 --> 00:01:59.029
In Java and in Kotlin, the wrapper is fromCallable, then subscribeOn(boundedElastic), on every inner Mono.

27
00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:02.205
And cap the fan-out with flatMap's concurrency.

28
00:02:03.033 --> 00:02:05.378
So publishOn moves everything after it.

29
00:02:05.378 --> 00:02:10.428
subscribeOn moves where the source runs, and only the one nearest the source counts.

30
00:02:10.633 --> 00:02:11.435
Quick check.

31
00:02:11.435 --> 00:02:17.517
subscribeOn(single) right after the source, publishOn(parallel) just before the subscriber.

32
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:19.656
Where does a map in between run?
